{{ template "chart.header" . }}

Chart version {{ template "chart.version" . }}, for SiVa {{ template "chart.appVersion" . }} (the two are always equal, see "Versioning").

Reference deployment of [SiVa](https://github.com/open-eid/SiVa), the Estonian digital-signature
validation service, on Kubernetes. Like the project's docker-compose file it is a reference, not a
product: best-effort, no support promise, build and run it yourself.

Licence: EUPL 1.1, the same as the SiVa repository this chart belongs with.

Pods are disposable. In snapshot mode (`tsl.mode: snapshot`, see "Trust lists") a new pod loads
the EU trust lists from a shared store and is ready in seconds with no download of its own, so
the deployment is friendly to the descheduler, to daily rolling restarts, to autoscaling and to
preemptible nodes; every replica validates against the same anchors. The pods carry the
safe-to-evict annotations the descheduler and the cluster autoscaler look for (`evictable`),
and the PodDisruptionBudget bounds how many go at once; which descheduler, and on what policy,
is yours to choose. Without ReadWriteMany storage the store lives in a bucket that the chart's
rclone containers mirror, never a CSI or FUSE mount (`tsl.snapshot.sync`).

## Before you install

- **Build your own image.** SiVa publishes source only. `./mvnw spring-boot:build-image` in the
  SiVa checkout produces a Paketo image of `siva-webapp`; push it to a registry your cluster can
  pull from and set `image.repository` to its full name there (`registry.example.invalid/siva-webapp`)
  and `image.tag`. The default `siva-webapp` names no registry: it keeps the chart rendering
  without one, and the install notes warn while it stands. `image.digest` pins the image by
  content (`repository:tag@sha256:...`); the rclone image of `tsl.snapshot.sync` and the
  `helm test` pod's busybox take a `digest` the same way.
- **In front of the pods.** `runtime.forwardHeadersStrategy` is `framework` by default: SiVa
  takes the client address and scheme (for its logs and the validation report) from the
  `X-Forwarded-*` headers of whatever connects to the pod. That is right behind an ingress
  controller or edge proxy that sets them, provided only that proxy reaches the traffic port:
  list its namespace or pods in `network.networkPolicy.ingressFrom` (with
  `network.networkPolicy.enabled`). Reachable from elsewhere (a NodePort, a LoadBalancer, an
  open policy), every client chooses what it is logged as; the install notes say so while the
  policy is open. When the proxy addresses are known, `native` is the alternative: Tomcat's
  RemoteIpValve honours the headers only from `server.tomcat.remoteip.internal-proxies` (a
  regular expression over the proxy addresses, set through `config.spring`; Spring Boot's
  default covers the private ranges). `none` when nothing in front sets them.
- **The request id.** Have the edge set or forward `X-Request-ID`: SiVa takes an incoming
  `X-Request-ID` first, then the trace-id of a W3C `traceparent`, mints one otherwise, and echoes
  the id as `X-Request-ID` on every response, so the edge's access log, the pod log (every line
  of a request carries it, the stage lines included) and the client's ticket join on one value.
  ingress-nginx sets the header from its `$req_id`; Gateway API implementations vary, so check
  yours. The id is logged as received: an opaque value, never a user or session identifier.
- **The two edge numbers.** Whatever sits in front of the pods must accept request bodies of
  27 MiB (SiVa's `siva.http.request.max-request-size-limit`: a 20 MB file plus its base64
  overhead; the edge answers 413 first if it allows less) and wait for the slowest validation
  before giving up on the upstream (or it answers 504 for a validation SiVa finishes). Both are
  values, `network.edge.maxBodyMiB` and `network.edge.timeoutSeconds` (0 = the drain timeout,
  `runtime.shutdownTimeout`, which is sized by the same number), the install notes print them,
  an entry in `network.httpRoutes` renders the wait as the route's `timeouts.request` (the body
  limit has no Gateway API field: set it on the Gateway's implementation), and an entry in
  `network.ingresses` with `edgeAnnotations: nginx` renders both as the ingress-nginx
  annotations (`proxy-body-size`, `proxy-read-timeout`, `proxy-send-timeout`); for another
  controller or a load balancer in front, set its own spelling of the two.
- **Routing.** `network.httpRoutes` renders Gateway API HTTPRoutes (gateway.networking.k8s.io/v1,
  the standard channel; v1.1 or later, since a v1.0 API server prunes the route's
  `timeouts.request` silently) that forward to the traffic Service, one per entry with its parent
  Gateway, hostnames and path matches; this is the model to prefer, Ingress being frozen
  upstream. `network.ingresses` renders Ingress objects for clusters that still route that way.
  Neither carries the management port.
- **Egress.** In the default `tsl.mode: online` each pod downloads the EU LOTL and the
  member-state trust lists at startup and on a nightly cron; in snapshot mode only the fetcher
  does (see "Trust lists"). Allow HTTPS egress to those hosts from whichever pods download, and
  from every webapp pod to the AIA and OCSP URLs of the certificates you validate and the policy
  URLs signatures name: the public internet, in practice (what the webapp connects to, and when,
  is under "Trust lists").
- **Trust material.** By default the pods use the truststores shipped on the application
  classpath (the CAs of the trust-list hosts, the LOTL signers), and rotating them means a new
  image. `trust.*` mounts the same material as PEM files instead and rotates them in place; see
  "Trust material".

```sh
helm install siva ./helm/siva-webapp \
  --set image.repository=registry.example.invalid/siva-webapp --set-string image.tag=3.10.3
```

`image.tag` and `env[].value` are strings; use `--set-string` for numeric-looking values (a git
short sha such as `1234567` would otherwise be parsed as a number and rejected by the values
schema).

## Configuration

`config.spring` is a Spring Boot configuration override in application.yml syntax, rendered as
the ConfigMap file `application.yml` and named to SiVa through
`SPRING_CONFIG_ADDITIONAL_LOCATION`, as the project's docker-compose setup does. It is a map, so
one key can be set from the command line and two overlays merge key by key:

```sh
helm upgrade siva ./helm/siva-webapp --reuse-values \
  --set config.spring.siva.tsl.loader.trusted-territories='{EE,LV,LT}'
```

```yaml
config:
  spring:
    siva:
      tsl:
        loader:
          trusted-territories: [EE, LV, LT]
    logging:
      level:
        ee.openeid: DEBUG
```

Write keys nested, in the kebab-case Spring Boot documents (`max-request-size-limit`); a dotted
key on one line survives `toYaml` but not `--set`, and Spring's relaxed binding accepts the
camelCase spelling of the SiVa deployment guide as well. Quote values that YAML would read as a
number or boolean when the property is a string. The values schema (`values.schema.json`)
checks every key against the configuration metadata the SiVa build emits (SiVa's own
`siva.*` and `t-level-signature-filter.*`, and Spring Boot's `server`, `management`, `logging`
and `spring`), so a mistyped key fails `helm lint`, `template`, `install` and `upgrade` naming
the key; a prefix nothing declares (`dss.*`) passes. The fragment is generated from the SiVa
build and committed, so an upgrade of SiVa regenerates it. Secrets never go into `config.spring`:
a ConfigMap is not a Secret; `secrets.existingSecret` sets the same properties from the files of
a mounted Secret (see "Secrets"), and the chart refuses to render a key here whose name ends in
`password`, `passwd`, `secret`, `token`, `api-key` or `private-key` (in either spelling:
`siva.signature-service.pkcs12.password`, `server.ssl.keyStorePassword`), naming the file to
put in that Secret instead; `logging.level.*` names packages and is exempt. The chart's own settings
(`runtime.*`, `tsl.*`, `trust.*`) are injected as environment variables, which win over the file,
so an override here cannot drop them.

`config.files` renders further files into the same ConfigMap, mounted read-only beside
`application.yml` at `config.mountPath`; a change to either rolls the pods through the
`checksum/config` annotation. The two the docker-compose setup mounts:

```yaml
config:
  spring:
    logging:
      config: /etc/siva-webapp/logback.xml
    siva:
      bdoc:
        digidoc4j-configuration-file: file:/etc/siva-webapp/siva-digidoc4j.yaml
  files:
    logback.xml: |
      <configuration>
        <include resource="org/springframework/boot/logging/logback/base.xml"/>
        ...
      </configuration>
    siva-digidoc4j.yaml: |
      # the whole digidoc4j configuration: it replaces the bundled siva-digidoc4j.yaml
      ...
```

`extraVolumes`, `extraVolumeMounts` and `extraContainers` pass pod spec through for what the
chart does not model (a sidecar, a mounted Secret the container reads itself), on the webapp
Deployment only; the fetcher pods take `tsl.fetcher.extraVolumes` and
`tsl.fetcher.extraVolumeMounts` of their own (a proxy's CA, a cloud credential file) and no
extra containers, since a Job completes only when every container has exited. The pods run
under the Pod Security "restricted" profile, so an extra container
needs its own `securityContext` (no privilege escalation, all capabilities dropped, read-only
root filesystem); with `network.networkPolicy.egress` on it also needs its own
`additionalRules`. No pod gets the namespace's Services as link variables
(`security.enableServiceLinks: false`; `<SERVICE>_SERVICE_HOST`, `<SERVICE>_PORT` and the rest,
which Kubernetes injects by default): SiVa reads none of them, and Spring's relaxed binding
would read them as properties, a Service named `server` setting `server.port` to `tcp://...`
and any `siva-*` Service landing under `siva.*`. Switch it on only for an extra container that
discovers Services that way.

A change to `config.spring` or `config.files` rolls the pods: the pod template carries a
`checksum/config` annotation over the rendered ConfigMap (the fetcher's template too, so the
bootstrap Job runs again). The ConfigMap itself is replaced in place, so the files in the pods
still running change as well, before the rollout reaches them. SiVa reads `application.yml` at
startup, so a running pod keeps its configuration; but a file it re-reads at runtime (a Logback
configuration with `scan="true"`) switches early, and a pod restarted for another reason while
a rollout is failing starts on the new files. Roll back with `helm rollback` (or a sync to the
previous revision in your GitOps tool), which restores the ConfigMap content together with the
annotation; `kubectl rollout undo` restores only the annotation and starts the "old" pods on the
new configuration.

## Secrets

Secrets are mounted as files, never put into the environment. `secrets.existingSecret` names a
Secret, not created by the chart, that is mounted read-only at `secrets.mountPath`
(`/etc/siva/secrets`) and imported as a Spring Boot config tree
(`SPRING_CONFIG_IMPORT=configtree:/etc/siva/secrets/`): every key of the Secret is a file whose
name is the property it sets, so the keys are named like properties and `config.spring` needs
no placeholder. The report-signing keystore goes into the same Secret as a file, and
`config.spring` names its path:

```yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Secret
metadata:
  name: siva-secrets
stringData:
  siva.signature-service.pkcs12.password: "..."
data:
  siva-signing.p12: <the PKCS12 file, base64>
```

```yaml
secrets:
  existingSecret: siva-secrets
config:
  spring:
    siva:
      signature-service:
        pkcs12:
          path: /etc/siva/secrets/siva-signing.p12
```

A file that is not a property (the keystore) is a property nobody reads. The same key written
into `config.spring` would land in a ConfigMap, so the chart refuses to render one named like a
secret (see "Configuration"). A value in the Secret
wins over the same key in `config.spring`; the chart's own environment variables (`runtime.*`,
`tsl.*`, `trust.*`) win over both. Whatever writes the Secret is outside the chart; with the
External Secrets Operator an `ExternalSecret` produces exactly this layout:

```yaml
apiVersion: external-secrets.io/v1   # v1beta1 on an operator before 0.17
kind: ExternalSecret
metadata:
  name: siva-secrets
spec:
  refreshInterval: 1h
  secretStoreRef:
    kind: ClusterSecretStore
    name: my-store
  target:
    name: siva-secrets               # what secrets.existingSecret names
  data:
    - secretKey: siva.signature-service.pkcs12.password
      remoteRef:
        key: siva/signing
        property: password
    - secretKey: siva-signing.p12
      remoteRef:
        key: siva/signing
        property: keystore
        decodingStrategy: Base64     # the store holds the file base64-encoded
```

Rotation: the files in a pod follow the Secret within the kubelet's sync period (about a
minute), but the process read them at startup, so a changed Secret reaches SiVa with the next
pod start. The chart renders no checksum for an object it does not render, so that rollout is
yours: `kubectl rollout restart deployment/<release>-siva-webapp` after rotating, the next
`helm upgrade`, or, if you run Stakater's Reloader, `podAnnotations:
{reloader.stakater.com/auto: "true"}`; nothing in the chart depends on it. Until then a pod
serves with the old value while its files already show the new one. The signing key rotates
with its certificate, so this is rare. The fetcher pods get a Secret of their own the same way
(`tsl.fetcher.existingSecret`), never the webapp's: see "Trust lists".

## Trust lists

SiVa validates against the EU list of trusted lists and the member-state lists it points to.
The chart offers two ways to get them into the pods:

- `tsl.mode: online` (default): every pod downloads them itself at startup and on SiVa's
  nightly cron. Simple, needs egress from every pod, takes minutes before a pod is ready, and
  replicas started at different times can hold different trust states for a while.
- `tsl.mode: snapshot`: a CronJob (`<release>-tsl-fetch`) runs the same image in SiVa's
  `tsl-fetch` profile, downloads the lists into a working cache and publishes them as a
  snapshot (`snapshots/<id>/` plus `current`) into a shared volume; the webapp pods mount it
  read-only, load the snapshot `current` names and poll for a new one
  (`tsl.snapshot.pollInterval`). A fresh pod is ready in seconds, every replica validates
  against the same anchors, and the webapp needs no egress to the list hosts. The fetcher
  publishes only when the mandatory territories (`siva.tsl.loader.mandatoryTerritories`) came
  through, so `current` always names the last good snapshot; a list whose download fails keeps
  its last copy in the working cache, and a run that had to use one still publishes but fails
  its Job (see "Fetch Job outcomes" below). A Job runs one fetch at install (and whenever the
  fetcher pod changes), so the pods have something to load before the first schedule. Needs a
  SiVa build with the fetcher (3.11.0 and later).

Snapshot mode needs a store the fetcher can write and every webapp pod can read: a
ReadWriteMany claim you bring (`tsl.snapshot.volume`), one the chart creates
(`tsl.snapshot.persistentVolumeClaim.create`, ReadWriteOnce is enough on a single node), or a
bucket (`tsl.snapshot.sync`, below). The fetcher renames files and directories in a volume
store, so its filesystem must support rename; an object-store mount without rename
(mountpoint-s3) cannot hold it, which is what the sync is for. The fetcher gets the same
ConfigMap, `env` and secret as the webapp, so its snapshot matches what the webapp expects
(same profile, same territories); `tsl.fetcher.env` adds fetcher-only variables. Set the
countries in `tsl.territories` so both get them.

Clusters without ReadWriteMany storage but with object storage (S3, Azure Blob, GCS, Swift:
anything rclone speaks) keep the store in a bucket instead (`tsl.snapshot.sync`). SiVa has no
object-storage client by design and keeps reading and writing a directory; the chart adds
rclone containers that mirror that directory and the bucket, which holds the store in the same
layout (`current`, `snapshots/<id>/`, `work/`):

- In the fetcher pod an init container downloads the bucket into a pod-local store before the
  fetch, so the fetch sees the last snapshot and its working cache (a list whose download fails
  keeps its last copy), and a second container uploads the result after it: `snapshots/` first,
  `current` last, the working cache after. It knows the fetch is over when the fetcher's JVM is
  gone (the fetcher pod shares its process namespace for that), so a run that publishes from a
  last copy (exit 3) is uploaded before its Job fails.
- In every webapp pod a sidecar (a restartable init container on Kubernetes 1.29+, a plain
  container below) reads the bucket's `current` every `interval` seconds, downloads the
  snapshot it names and, once every file the manifest counts is there, points the pod's own
  `current` at it; SiVa polls that file as it does on a shared volume. A snapshot still being
  uploaded is never current, and SiVa refuses a snapshot missing a file its manifest lists as
  well, so a copy without rename atomicity cannot feed a pod half a snapshot.

```yaml
tsl:
  mode: snapshot
  snapshot:
    sync:
      enabled: true
      remote: bucket:siva/tsl          # <remote>:<bucket>/<prefix>
      existingSecret: siva-tsl-rclone  # key rclone.conf: the [bucket] section and its keys
      interval: 60
```

The rclone configuration comes as a file, never as environment: a Secret with the key
`rclone.conf` for static keys, or `config` (rendered into a ConfigMap) when it holds none
because the cloud's workload identity or instance role signs the requests (`env_auth = true`;
bind the identity with write access to the fetcher's ServiceAccount,
`tsl.fetcher.serviceAccount.annotations`, and one with read access to the webapp's). `env`
passes rclone flags as `RCLONE_*` variables (`RCLONE_S3_NO_CHECK_BUCKET=true` for keys that
may not create the bucket), the cloud's identity variables or a proxy. The sync containers run
rclone's own image as the SiVa image's uid (`runAsUser`, `runAsGroup`: the fetcher writes into
the directory the init container downloaded) under the chart's restricted security context,
and the deployer updates that image on their own schedule. With `network.networkPolicy.egress`
on, add the bucket endpoint to `additionalRules` unless it is on the public internet. Freshness
on a pod is `interval` plus `tsl.snapshot.pollInterval`, and the chart adds `interval` to the
alerts' grace. The store lock cannot serialise two fetchers in bucket mode (each run has its
own copy of the store): the CronJob forbids overlapping runs of its own, and the bootstrap Job
overlapping a scheduled run means two uploads of complete snapshots, the later one winning.

`tsl.fetcher.period` (six hours by default, at most a week) is the one setting the rest follows:
the CronJob schedule and `tsl.snapshot.maxAge` (max(4 x period, 48 h)) derive from it, and the
fetcher writes it into every snapshot, from where the pods publish it as
`siva_tsl_refresh_period_seconds`; the trust-list alerts take their thresholds from that gauge
(two periods plus `monitoring.prometheusRule.tsl.grace` for a stale snapshot or a list served
from its last copy, two periods plus the lead time before a list expires), so the rules carry
no period of their own and hold for a plain-cron deployment that sets `siva.tsl.fetcher.period`
itself. A snapshot that goes stale degrades every replica at once, and member-state lists run
out within three to six months of issue, which is why a period beyond a week is refused. The
deployment guide's "Choosing the fetch period" has the table.

### Fetch Job outcomes

The fetcher's exit code is the Job's result, and the Job is what the cluster's own alerts
watch (kube-prometheus-stack's `KubeJobFailed`), so the chart adds no fetch alert of its own:

| Exit | Meaning | Job |
|---|---|---|
| 0 | Every list downloaded, snapshot published | Complete |
| 1 | A mandatory territory's list is missing, expired or badly signed; nothing published, `current` unchanged | Failed at once |
| 2 | The store could not be written | Failed after `tsl.fetcher.backoffLimit` retries |
| 3 | A list's download failed and the run published its last good copy (the fetch log's `using the last copy on disk` line names the list and the reason) | Failed at once |
| 4 | Another fetch held the store's lock for the whole ten-minute wait; nothing downloaded, nothing published | Failed after `tsl.fetcher.backoffLimit` retries |

Exit 1 and 3 fail for reasons a retry seconds later cannot change, so a `podFailurePolicy`
fails the Job without the usual retries (Kubernetes 1.26 and later, the chart's floor). One
fetcher runs per store at a time: the CronJob forbids overlapping runs of its own, and the
fetcher takes a lock file in the store (`fetch.lock`) so that the bootstrap Job, or a run
started by hand, waits for a running fetch instead of sharing its working cache; a lock older
than an hour is taken over as left by a run that was killed. After
exit 3 the pods keep validating on good lists and `SivaTslListNotDownloaded` says which list
has not been downloaded for two periods; the next successful run clears it. `KubeJobFailed`
stays on until the Failed Job object is gone: the history limits rotate failed Jobs out only
as newer ones fail, so after an outage delete the Job once you have read its log, or set
`tsl.fetcher.ttlSecondsAfterFinished` to have them expire (then the log goes with them). The
bootstrap Job gets no TTL: it is a tracked resource, and a GitOps controller would recreate
and re-run it.

Whoever can write the store can point every pod at any snapshot whose lists are still inside
their validity window (a rollback or a freeze; forging a list is not possible, the pods verify
every list's signature against the LOTL signers), so treat write access to it as trust-list
authority: only the fetcher writes, the webapp mounts it read-only. `tsl.snapshot.maxAge` is
not a defence against such a writer: the age it checks is the manifest's own `createdAt`,
which the writer sets. It makes a pod refuse a snapshot from a fetcher that stopped
running rather than serve it. The fetcher pods get their own
ServiceAccount (`tsl.fetcher.serviceAccount`), so an identity that may write the store (a
cloud role for the bucket) is never the webapp's, and their own
Secret (`tsl.fetcher.existingSecret`, mounted as under "Secrets"), never the webapp's: they parse
thirty documents from the internet and should hold nothing else. The store holds nothing worth
backing up: a lost store is rebuilt by the next fetch, so leave the claim out of your backup
scope.

A fetch resolves some thirty public hostnames and nothing in the cluster but DNS itself, and
under the cluster default `ndots:5` each name with fewer than five dots (`ec.europa.eu` has
two) is first tried with every search domain appended (`<name>.<namespace>.svc.cluster.local`,
`<name>.svc.cluster.local`, `<name>.cluster.local`, the node's own), one round trip to the
cluster DNS each, before the name itself is asked. `tsl.fetcher.dnsConfig` is the pod's
`dnsConfig`, rendered as given into the CronJob and the bootstrap Job; this sends every name
with two dots or more straight out and leaves a one-word Service name on the search list:

```yaml
tsl:
  fetcher:
    dnsConfig:
      options:
        - name: ndots
          value: "2"
```

The webapp pods keep the cluster default: a sidecar or an extra container of yours may count
on it, and the AIA, OCSP and policy hosts a validation resolves are few per request.

What a webapp pod fetches while validating comes with the document: missing issuer
certificates from the AIA URLs the certificates carry, the signature policy from the URL a
signature names, and OCSP responses for chains that reach a trusted-list anchor, the latter only
when the T-level country filter is configured. CRLs are never fetched, and validation makes no
timestamp request (report signing, off by default, is the only TSA or OCSP client, at
operator-chosen URLs). With `network.networkPolicy.egress` on, the webapp pods reach DNS (the
`kube-system` namespace by default, `egress.dnsTo`) and the public internet on 80 and 443,
never the private ranges, the carrier-grade NAT range or the link-local range (`169.254.0.0/16`,
where the cloud providers' metadata endpoints live), and the fetcher pods the same with no
ingress at all; tighten `to` to your responders' addresses if your CNI cannot resolve names, or
route through an egress proxy with `additionalRules`.

## Trust material

Two sets of certificates decide what the pods trust before any signature is validated: the CAs
of the hosts the trust lists are downloaded from (TLS), and the certificates the LOTL is signed
with (announced in the Official Journal). SiVa ships both as PKCS12 files on its classpath.
`trust.*` replaces them with PEM files mounted at `trust.mountPath`: `trust.pem` renders the
files into the ConfigMap `<release>-trust`, `trust.existingConfigMap` or `trust.existingSecret`
takes an object something else maintains (a trust-manager `Bundle` with a ConfigMap or Secret
target writes exactly this shape: one concatenated PEM per key). The chart wires each file as a
Spring SSL bundle with `reload-on-update` and names it to SiVa (`siva.tsl.loader.sslBundle`,
`lotlBundle`; SiVa 3.11.0 and later), for the webapp and the fetcher alike. A changed file
reaches the pods within the kubelet's sync period, Spring Boot reloads the bundle and logs it,
and the trust lists use it from the next download or refresh: no restart, no image. A file that
does not parse replaces the bundle all the same (Spring Boot swaps it before it runs its update
handlers): the loaders log the reload error, the next download or snapshot load fails until the
file is fixed, a fetcher started on it fails its Job, and the lists already loaded stay in use.
It reaches every pod within the minute, so validate the material before you publish it.

The CA file is the whole trust for the trust-list downloads, as the classpath truststore is
today, so it must hold every CA those hosts chain to. It does not touch the JVM's own
truststore, which the OCSP, AIA and timestamp clients use; `trust.caCertificatesBinding`
hands the same file to Paketo's ca-certificates helper, which adds it to the JVM truststore at
launch (Paketo images only, pod restart on change).

## Versioning

The chart has no releases of its own: `version` and `appVersion` are the SiVa version (the Maven
project version, `-SNAPSHOT` included) and change only in SiVa's own version-bump commit. The
chart from a SiVa checkout is therefore the chart for that SiVa, and `image.tag` left empty
runs that version.

Internal builds get a build id, generated at build time and never committed:
`<SiVa version without its pre-release>-dev.<UTC yyyymmddTHHMMSS>.g<short sha>`, for example
`3.11.0-dev.20261019T084942.g597535b`. It is both the image tag and the version of the chart
package pushed with it, so one build is one identifier everywhere. `dev` is constant, the
fixed-width timestamp is a single alphanumeric identifier that semver compares lexically, so
later builds sort higher (`T` keeps it alphanumeric: a numeric identifier with a leading zero
would be invalid), and the sha is for tracing. A tool that tracks "the newest version" must
admit pre-releases (a semver constraint such as `>=0.0.0-0`; a bare `*` matches release
versions only).

