## Verifying on a NodePort

```sh
helm install siva ./helm/siva-webapp --set image.repository=<your-registry>/siva-webapp \
  --set service.type=NodePort --set service.nodePort=30080
kubectl rollout status deployment/siva-siva-webapp --timeout=15m   # online mode downloads the trust lists first
curl -X POST -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
     --data @build-helpers/sample-requests/<any>.json http://<node>:30080/validate
helm test siva
```

## Dashboards and alerts that pair with SiVa

The chart ships only what needs SiVa's knowledge: the trust-list and error-rate alerts
(`monitoring.prometheusRule`) and one overview dashboard (`monitoring.grafanaDashboard`). The
ServiceMonitor and the PrometheusRule need the Prometheus Operator CRDs
(`monitoring.coreos.com/v1`): the chart renders them whenever they are enabled, and without the
CRDs the API server refuses the install with "no matches for kind ServiceMonitor". For the
rest, use what your monitoring stack and the Grafana community already maintain:

| Need | Use | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| JVM in depth | [JVM (Micrometer)](https://grafana.com/grafana/dashboards/4701), ID 4701 | 24 of its 27 panels fill against SiVa 3.11. Thread utilisation needs `server.tomcat.mbeanregistry.enabled: true` in `config.spring`; process memory needs micrometer-jvm-extras, which SiVa does not include; the errors panel stays empty until a request fails with 5xx. |
| Spring Boot overview by namespace | [Spring Boot 3.x Statistics](https://grafana.com/grafana/dashboards/19004), ID 19004 | Its six HikariCP panels stay empty: SiVa has no database. |
| Pod CPU, memory against limits, restarts | kube-prometheus-stack's "Kubernetes / Compute Resources / Pod" | Ships with the stack. |
| Pod not ready, crash loops | kube-prometheus-stack's `KubePodNotReady`, `KubePodCrashLooping` | The chart does not repeat them; `SivaOomKilled` stays, the stack sees an OOM kill only as a crash loop. |
| A fetch Job that failed | kube-prometheus-stack's `KubeJobFailed` | The fetcher fails its Job when it could not fetch (see "Fetch Job outcomes"); the chart's `SivaTslListNotDownloaded` then names the list. |

Both community dashboards select by an `application` label. `monitoring.serviceMonitor` adds
it (the release's full name) unless `applicationLabel` is false; without the operator, set
`management.metrics.tags.application` in SiVa's config instead.

The chart's own dashboard reads SiVa's validation timer, `siva_validation_seconds` (one
observation per `/validate` or `/validateHashcode` request, labels `request`, `report_type`,
`container_type` and `error`; the deployment guide, "The stages of a request"), as a mean by
report type. SiVa publishes it as `_count`, `_sum` and `_max` only; percentiles need the
histogram buckets, which one Spring Boot switch in `config.spring` adds:

```yaml
config:
  spring:
    management:
      metrics:
        distribution:
          percentiles-histogram:
            siva:
              validation: true
```

That turns `siva_validation_seconds` (and, by prefix, `siva_validation_service_seconds`, the
per-validator timer) into `siva_validation_seconds_bucket` series for
`histogram_quantile(0.95, sum by (le, report_type) (rate(siva_validation_seconds_bucket[5m])))`,
at the cost of one series per bucket and label combination.

### Alert runbooks

Every rule carries its runbook as the `runbook` annotation (what the alert means, the first
check, the usual cause, what clears it), so it reaches whoever receives the page; the table
is the same text, rendered from the rules at the chart's default periods (the durations in
"Fires when" follow `tsl.fetcher.period`, `monitoring.prometheusRule.tsl.grace` and `leadTime`
on your install). Each live rule has been fired once by fault injection and seen to clear;
the two behind `pendingRules` wait for their signals.

<!-- runbooks:begin -->
| Alert | Severity | Fires when | Runbook |
|---|---|---|---|
| `SivaValidationErrorRate` | warning | SiVa 5xx ratio above 5 % for 10 minutes | More than 5 % of the requests the traffic port answered in the last 10 minutes were 5xx; the kubelet's probe requests (/readyz, /livez) count among them, so on a quiet instance a few failures are enough, and a pod that is not ready answers its probes 503, which count as failures. First check: split the ratio by pod and uri (sum by (pod, uri) (rate(http_server_requests_seconds_count{namespace="<namespace>",status=~"5.."}[10m]))) and read the pods' 'Unexpected error' lines, each with the request id the client got back. Usual cause: a client sending what the validation cannot process (a hashcode request whose signature file is not XAdES is answered 500), or one pod failing every request after a bad reload. Clears ten minutes after the last 5xx. |
| `SivaOomKilled` | critical | SiVa pod <pod> was OOM-killed in the last 30 minutes | The kernel killed the container because heap plus everything the JVM holds outside it (metaspace, thread stacks, code cache, GC structures) exceeded resources.limits.memory, and the requests in flight on it were lost. First check: kubectl -n <namespace> describe pod <pod> (Last State OOMKilled, exit code 137) and the pod's memory before the kill in the cluster's Pod dashboard. Usual cause: a limit lowered below what the workload needs, more Tomcat threads than Paketo's memory calculator was told (BPL_JVM_THREAD_COUNT), or a large document under load. A limit below the calculator's fixed regions does not reach the kernel: the JVM refuses to start (exit 82, reason Error) and the cluster's crash-loop rule fires instead. Clears once the pod's last termination is no longer OOMKilled, that is with the next restart or replacement: raise the limit (the heap is sized from it) or lower the thread count and roll. |
| `SivaTslMandatoryTerritoryNotValidating` | critical | SiVa holds no trust anchors for mandatory territory <territory> (<loader> loader) | For 15 minutes the pod has held no trust anchor for a territory siva.tsl.loader.mandatoryTerritories names: it is not ready (health group tsl DOWN) and signatures from that territory validate as untrusted on it. First check: /monitoring/health on the management port (missingMandatoryTerritories lists them) and whether every pod shows the same territory. Usual cause: a code with no list in the LOTL SiVa reads (EE is EE_T under the test profile), its list expired or badly signed, or in snapshot mode a fetcher that could not publish (its Job fails with exit 1 and the cluster's failed-Job rule fires); a rollout then stalls on a new pod that never becomes ready while the old pods keep serving. Clears as soon as the pod holds anchors for the territory again (a corrected configuration rolled out, a new list fetched) or the pod is replaced. |
| `SivaTslTerritoryNotValidating` | warning | SiVa holds no trust anchors for territory <territory> (<loader> loader) | For 15 minutes the pod has held no trust anchor for a trusted, non-mandatory territory; it stays ready, and signatures from that territory validate as untrusted. First check: the tsl health detail on the management port and the fetch (or pod) log for the list's download or signature error. Usual cause: a territory code with no list in the LOTL, or a list the publisher let expire or signed with a key the LOTL does not name. Clears as soon as the pod holds anchors for it again; make the territory mandatory if its signatures must never validate as untrusted. |
| `SivaTslListExpiringSoon` | warning | The <territory> trusted list (<kind>) runs out within two refresh periods plus 7 d | The list's NextUpdate is less than two refresh periods plus the lead time away and no newer version has reached the pod; nothing is broken yet. First check: whether the publisher has issued a newer list (the LOTL for the EU list, the territory's scheme operator for a national one) and the fetch Job's or pod's log for its download; compare siva_tsl_next_update_timestamp_seconds across pods. Usual cause: the publisher is late, or the fetch has not run or not downloaded since it published (SivaTslSnapshotStale and SivaTslListNotDownloaded say which). Clears when the pod holds a list with a later NextUpdate. |
| `SivaTslListExpiring` | critical | The <territory> trusted list (<kind>) runs out within one refresh period plus a day | The list's NextUpdate is less than one refresh period plus a day away. Once it passes, signatures relying on the list lose qualification (POLv4 fails them) and, at the pod's next reload, their trust anchors: the territory then alerts as not validating. First check: as for SivaTslListExpiringSoon, now also whether the fetch would pick a newer list up in time (the CronJob's next run). Usual cause: a publisher that let the list run out, or a fetch that stopped (SivaTslSnapshotStale) or cannot download (SivaTslListNotDownloaded). Clears when the pod holds a list with a later NextUpdate. |
| `SivaTslListNotDownloaded` | warning | SiVa serves the <territory> trusted list (<kind>) from a copy older than two refresh periods plus 1 h | Every download of the list in the last two refresh periods plus the grace failed and SiVa serves it from its last good copy; the fetch itself ran (SivaTslSnapshotStale is quiet) or, if that fires too, stopped. First check: the newest fetch Job's log (kubectl -n <namespace> logs job/<newest <release>-tsl-fetch-...>; the Job is Failed with exit 3) or the pod's log in online mode for the 'using the last copy on disk' line, which names the URL and the error. Usual cause: the publisher unreachable (DNS, egress, a proxy, a redirect to a maintenance page) or a TLS certificate the tsl bundle does not trust. Clears at the first successful download; the copy stays valid until the list's NextUpdate, which SivaTslListExpiring watches. |
| `SivaTslSnapshotStale` | warning | SiVa pod <pod> runs on a snapshot older than two fetch periods plus 1 h | The snapshot the pod runs on was published more than two fetch periods plus the grace ago: no newer one reached it. SivaTslListNotDownloaded fires with it when the fetcher stopped (the download times age too) and stays quiet when snapshots keep arriving elsewhere. First check: kubectl -n <namespace> get cronjob,jobs (suspended, schedule, failing Jobs) and the newest Job's log; then the store's current against the pod's log for a refused snapshot (older than tsl.snapshot.maxAge, a file its manifest lists missing) and, with a bucket, the sync sidecar's log. Usual cause: the CronJob suspended or its schedule not firing, a Job failing (exit 1: a mandatory territory missing; exit 2: the store not writable; exit 4: the store's fetch.lock left behind by a fetch that was killed, taken over after an hour), a stalled sync, or the pod refusing what arrived. Clears when the pod loads a newer snapshot, within tsl.snapshot.pollInterval of its publication. |
| `SivaRequestRejectedSize` (behind `pendingRules`) | info | SiVa rejects oversized requests | A client sent a request above the size limit and was answered 413. First check: the pod log for the request id and the client's address or usrId header. Usual cause: a container above the documented 20 MB plus its base64 overhead. Clears 15 minutes after the last rejection; raise siva.http.request.max-request-size-limit together with network.edge.maxBodyMiB only if such documents are legitimate. |
| `SivaOcspTsaFailures` (behind `pendingRules`) | warning | OCSP or timestamp responders failing | OCSP responders or timestamp authorities failed for 15 minutes; LT and LTA validations degrade. First check: the pod log for the responder URLs and errors, then egress to them (NetworkPolicy, proxy, DNS). Usual cause: a responder down or unreachable from the cluster. Clears 15 minutes after the last failure. |
<!-- runbooks:end -->

Two of them read together: `SivaTslSnapshotStale` and `SivaTslListNotDownloaded` firing at
once say the fetcher has stopped (both ages grow); `SivaTslListNotDownloaded` alone says the
fetcher runs but the publisher is out of reach. `SivaValidationErrorRate` counts the kubelet's
probe requests on the traffic port among the requests, so a quiet instance needs only a few
5xx to cross 5 %.

## Known limits

- Readiness waits for the mandatory trusted lists (`siva.tsl.loader.mandatoryTerritories`, `EE`
  by default); every other territory can be missing or stale without taking the pod out of
  rotation; `SivaTslTerritoryNotValidating` (warning) says so, from SiVa's `siva_tsl_trust_anchors`
  gauge, and `SivaTslMandatoryTerritoryNotValidating` (critical) for the mandatory ones.
- In online mode every replica fetches the trust lists itself; replicas started at different
  times can hold different trust states until the next refresh. Snapshot mode (see "Trust
  lists") removes that, at the price of a shared volume and a CronJob.
- Metrics are at `<release>-management:8081/monitoring/prometheus` (the service port when
  `runtime.managementPort` equals `service.containerPort`); `monitoring.serviceMonitor` scrapes
  it with the Prometheus operator, otherwise scrape it yourself. The trust-list rules use SiVa's
  `siva_tsl_*` gauges, which exist for exactly the configured countries and mark the mandatory
  ones, so the rules carry no country list; request-size rejections and OCSP/TSA failures have
  no metric yet (`monitoring.prometheusRule.pendingRules`).
- The actuator lives on the management port (`8081` by default), reachable only inside the
  cluster through `<release>-management`. The traffic port serves SiVa's documented monitoring
  URLs (`/monitoring/health` with its groups, `/monitoring/heartbeat`, `/monitoring/version`,
  the same answers and status codes, 503 on DOWN; SiVa 3.11.1 and later, an older image answers
  404 there) and the status-only `/readyz` and `/livez`, which the kubelet probes and an
  external load balancer's health check use and which work with any image; `/monitoring/
  prometheus` answers 404 there. An Ingress that should not expose the detailed health lists
  the paths it allows. With `runtime.managementPort` equal to the traffic port the probes use
  `/monitoring/health/readiness` and `/liveness`.
- The preStop pause uses the kubelet's Sleep action on Kubernetes 1.30+ and needs nothing in
  the image. Below 1.30 it execs `sleep`, which Paketo's default tiny run image does not ship:
  there, use a run image that has it or set `runtime.preStopSleepSeconds: 0` (and accept that a
  rollout may refuse a request or two). `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` must exceed sleep +
  `runtime.shutdownTimeout`; the chart refuses to render otherwise. The sleep covers new
  connections only: an idle HTTP/1.1 keep-alive connection still held to the pod is cut when
  SIGTERM pauses Tomcat, and a request already on its way over it gets no answer (one lost
  request per idle connection per pod stop). Who holds such connections depends on what is in
  front: an L4 load balancer or a NodePort/ClusterIP path keeps established flows on the old pod
  (conntrack) and every pooled client connection is exposed; a request-aware balancer that
  watches endpoints (ingress-nginx, Envoy, an ALB target group) stops choosing the pod once its
  endpoint is gone and its idle pool entries are simply never reused. The chart cannot tell the
  two apart, so `runtime.httpKeepAlive` is off by default (`Connection: close` on every
  response), which is right in both. Measured cost at 8 concurrent validations, 600 s
  each way: +0.4 ms connect time per request, about four more packets each way, CPU within
  +0.6 to +1.8 % (121 ms per validation either way), memory unchanged, and one SYN lost in
  36000 requests cost that request a 1 s retransmit. Turn it on behind an endpoint-watching
  balancer after a rollout under load through that balancer shows no loss; keep it on if TLS
  ever terminates at the pod, where a handshake per request is no longer cheap.
- Put JVM flags in `runtime.javaToolOptions`, not in `env`: an `env` entry named
  `JAVA_TOOL_OPTIONS` replaces the chart's.
- A larger Tomcat pool needs Paketo told about it. Paketo's memory calculator sizes the heap
  from the memory limit after reserving one stack per thread it assumes, `BPL_JVM_THREAD_COUNT`
  (250 by default). Raising `server.tomcat.threads.max` above that (through `config.spring`; a
  deployment whose validations wait on slow external responders may want a larger pool)
  without raising `BPL_JVM_THREAD_COUNT` in `env` to match means the calculator does not
  account for the extra threads' stacks. Raising it takes the same share from the heap at an
  unchanged `resources.limits.memory` (1 MB per thread by default), and the calculator refuses
  to start when the limit cannot hold what it reserves. Set the two together and size the
  limit from them.
- Trust material comes from the classpath unless `trust.*` is set; the JVM's own truststore
  (OCSP, AIA, timestamp clients) is only reachable through the Paketo binding, at launch.
